IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `jcxv-dates`

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) -- the questions of a party to a civil proceeding.
--
-- SEAT: the party: usually the person bringing a claim (the plaintiff, applicant or claimant), whose proceeding is in a
-- Western Australian court, the Federal Court, the Family Court or a Supreme Court of another State or Territory, and who
-- meets the cross-vesting scheme when a transfer, a removal, an appeal or the enforcement of a judgment arises. "Does
-- this Act deal with my proceeding?", "Must my proceeding be transferred, and where?", "Can I apply?", "Can the Supreme
-- Court keep my special federal matter?", "Have I given the notice the court directed?", "Where does my appeal go?",
-- "Which law will the court apply?", "Can I appeal the transfer?". The party answering the claim asks the same questions
-- with the same answers. The courts and the Attorneys-General get no seat: what a court finds or is satisfied of is
-- asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline a party meets (to give the Attorneys-General written notice of a special federal
-- matter within the time the Supreme Court directs, s 6(4), (5)(b)) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole operative Act: s 3 (terms used, including s 3(2)), s 3A (Corporations Act), s 4 (vesting
-- of jurisdiction), s 5 (transfer of proceedings), s 6 (special federal matters), s 6A (Commonwealth authorities under
-- State laws), s 7 (appeals), s 8 (removal to the Supreme Court), s 9 (exercise of cross-vested jurisdiction; folded into
-- the answers of s 4 and s 5), s 10 (Trade Practices Act matters), s 11 (conduct of proceedings), s 12 (costs), s 13
-- (limitation on appeals), s 14(2), (3) (enforcement and effect of judgments). Out: s 1, s 2, the preamble, s 15 (reading down), s 16 (suspension by
-- proclamation): machinery. Reasons in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA), Act No. 68 of 1987, compilation including the Legal
-- Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2022 s 424 (in force 1 July 2022), retrieved 7 September 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_45308.txt. No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS. Answers follow the
-- rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the
-- text's no)"; an answer where the text does not answer is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987: the questions of a party to a civil proceeding`

-- The courts the Act names. s 3(1): "Federal Court" is the Federal Court of Australia; "Family Court" is the Family
-- Court of Australia; the "State Family Court" of Western Australia is the Family Court of Western Australia (the court
-- to which s 41 of the Family Law Act 1975 applies by proclamation; borrowed, taken as that court). "State" includes the
-- Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory; "Territory" excludes them.
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of Western Australia`
    `the Family Court of Western Australia`
    `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory`
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Family Court of Australia`
    `another court or tribunal`

-- s 3(2): "A reference in this Act, other than a reference in section 4(3), (6) or (7), 5(3) or (4) or 7(4), to the
-- Supreme Court of a State includes, if there is a State Family Court of that State, a reference to that State Family
-- Court." So "the Supreme Court" in ss 5(1), (2), (5), 6, 6A, 7(3), (5), 8, 10 and 14 includes the Family Court of
-- Western Australia (fork F2).
GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Western Australia`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia`

GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Federal Court or the Family Court` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Family Court of Australia`

§§ `s 3 and s 3A -- Proceedings the Act deals with`

-- s 3(1): "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding". s 3A: "This Act does not apply to the jurisdiction of
-- courts with which Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 of the Commonwealth deals." Both are flat
-- negatives: the text's no.
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN, `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations` IF `criminal` OR `corporations`

DECLARE `Does this Act deal with my proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a civil proceeding, which this Act's rules on transfer, removal and appeal can reach (s 3)`
    `no: a criminal proceeding is not a "proceeding" under this Act (s 3(1), the text's no)`
    `no: this Act does not apply to the jurisdiction that Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 deals with (s 3A, the text's no)`

@export Does the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) deal with my proceeding at all (s 3, s 3A)? It does not reach criminal proceedings, or the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001.
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding (a prosecution), rather than a civil one
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court's jurisdiction in your proceeding is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
GIVETH A `Does this Act deal with my proceeding`
`s 3, s 3A -- does this Act deal with my proceeding` `criminal` `corporations` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `criminal` THEN `no: a criminal proceeding is not a "proceeding" under this Act (s 3(1), the text's no)`
        IF `corporations` THEN `no: this Act does not apply to the jurisdiction that Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 deals with (s 3A, the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: a civil proceeding, which this Act's rules on transfer, removal and appeal can reach (s 3)`

-- s 3(1): "State matter means a matter (a) in which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction otherwise than by reason of a
-- law of the Commonwealth or of another State; or (b) removed to the Supreme Court under section 8". A "means"
-- definition: when neither limb holds, the text's no.
GIVEN `own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN, `removed under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a State matter` `own jurisdiction` `removed under s 8` IF `own jurisdiction` OR `removed under s 8`

§§ `s 4 -- Vesting of additional jurisdiction in certain courts`

-- s 4(3): "The Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction
-- with respect to State matters." s 4(4): the same for "The State Family Court of another State". s 4(5): not in
-- criminal matters. s 9: the court may exercise that jurisdiction and hear a proceeding transferred to it. s 4(1) and
-- (2), which vested State jurisdiction in the federal courts, are deleted (finding 1).
DECLARE `The court outside Western Australia` IS ONE OF
    `a Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory`
    `a State Family Court of another State`
    `the Federal Court or the Family Court of Australia (a federal court)`

DECLARE `Can a court outside Western Australia hear my Western Australian matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: that Supreme Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your State matter (s 4(3))`
    `yes: that State Family Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your State matter (s 4(4))`
    `no: s 4 gives no jurisdiction in criminal matters (s 4(5), the text's no)`
    `no: this Act does not apply to the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A, the text's no) -- s 4`
    `s 4 gives it nothing: your matter is not a State matter (s 3(1)), and this Act does not say more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 4 gives it nothing: s 4(3) and (4) vest jurisdiction in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States and Territories, not in a federal court; the former s 4(1) and (2) are deleted (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can a court outside Western Australia hear my matter that arises under Western Australian law (s 4)? The Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, and a State Family Court of another State, have jurisdiction in "State matters"; not in criminal matters.
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is a criminal matter
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court of Western Australia has jurisdiction in your matter without relying on a law of the Commonwealth or of another State (for example, a claim under Western Australian law or the common law)
      `removed under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter was removed into the Supreme Court of Western Australia by an order under s 8
      `the court` IS A `The court outside Western Australia` @desc The court outside Western Australia that you want to hear your matter
GIVETH A `Can a court outside Western Australia hear my Western Australian matter`
`s 4 -- can a court outside Western Australia hear my matter` `criminal` `corporations` `own jurisdiction` `removed under s 8` `the court` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `criminal` THEN `no: s 4 gives no jurisdiction in criminal matters (s 4(5), the text's no)`
        IF `corporations` THEN `no: this Act does not apply to the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A, the text's no) -- s 4`
        IF NOT (`a State matter` `own jurisdiction` `removed under s 8`) THEN `s 4 gives it nothing: your matter is not a State matter (s 3(1)), and this Act does not say more (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `a Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` THEN `yes: that Supreme Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your State matter (s 4(3))`
        IF `the court` EQUALS `a State Family Court of another State` THEN `yes: that State Family Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your State matter (s 4(4))`
        OTHERWISE `s 4 gives it nothing: s 4(3) and (4) vest jurisdiction in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States and Territories, not in a federal court; the former s 4(1) and (2) are deleted (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 5 and s 6A -- Transfer of proceedings`

-- s 5(1)-(5): where a proceeding is pending in a first court and "it appears to" that court that it is more appropriate
-- (on the matters the subsection lists, or because the proceeding is related to one in the other court) or (s 5(2)-(5)
-- only) "otherwise in the interests of justice" that the proceeding be determined by the other court, "the first court
-- shall transfer" it. What the court finds is a judgment: asked as a fact. Fork F3: the related-proceeding limb (i) and
-- the listed-matters limb (ii) both end in "it is more appropriate", so one answer stands for both.
DECLARE `What the court finds about where it should be decided` IS ONE OF
    `the court finds it more appropriate that the other court decide it`
    `the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide it`
    `the court finds neither`

-- s 6A(1): a "federal matter proceeding": (a) a matter in it is covered by paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth Act's
-- definition of special federal matter; (b) it involves the exercise of functions conferred on a Commonwealth authority or
-- officer by a State enactment referred to in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act's definition of enactment; (c) it
-- arises out of or relates to another proceeding pending in a Western Australian court (i) arising, or substantially
-- arising, under that State enactment or a corresponding one, and (ii) in which no matter is a paragraph (c) or (e)
-- special federal matter. The borrowed terms are asked as facts.
GIVEN `6A(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN, `6A(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN, `6A(1)(c)(i)` IS A BOOLEAN, `6A(1)(c)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6A(1) -- a federal matter proceeding` `6A(1)(a)` `6A(1)(b)` `6A(1)(c)(i)` `6A(1)(c)(ii)` IF
        `6A(1)(a)`
    AND `6A(1)(b)`
    AND `6A(1)(c)(i)`
    AND `6A(1)(c)(ii)`

DECLARE `Must my proceeding be transferred` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
    `yes: that Supreme Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Western Australia (s 5(3))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Supreme Court and the Family Court of Western Australia, which then has jurisdiction in every matter in it (s 5(4), s 4(6), (7))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the Western Australian court you name: the Supreme Court of Western Australia, or the Family Court of Western Australia, which s 3(2) reads into "the Supreme Court" (s 5(5))`
    `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not required by s 5(1) (s 6A(3), the text's no), but the Supreme Court may transfer it, and it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    `no: s 5(1) does not require it (s 6A(3), the text's no), and the court does not consider transfer appropriate`
    `may: s 5(5) does not apply (s 6A(2)), but the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court, and it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    `no: s 5(5) does not apply (s 6A(2), the text's no), and the court does not consider transfer appropriate`
    `this Act does not provide for that transfer; the cross-vesting Act of the Commonwealth or of another State may (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 5`

@export Must my proceeding be transferred to another court under the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) (s 5, s 6A)? Where the court finds it more appropriate (or, for most transfers, otherwise in the interests of justice) that another court decide it, the court must transfer it. A party may apply for the transfer, and the court may act of its own motion (s 5(8)). No appeal lies from the decision (s 13).
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `pending in` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in now
      `to go to` IS A `Court` @desc The court it might be transferred to
      `the finding` IS A `What the court finds about where it should be decided` @desc What the court finds: that it is more appropriate that the other court decide your proceeding (having regard to the matters s 5 lists, or because it is related to a proceeding there), or otherwise in the interests of justice, or neither
      `6A(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter of paragraph (c) or (e) of the definition in the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act (s 6A(1)(a))
      `6A(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that matter involves functions or powers given to a Commonwealth authority or officer by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act's definition of enactment (s 6A(1)(b))
      `6A(1)(c)(i)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding arises out of or relates to another proceeding pending in a Western Australian court that arises (or substantially arises) under that State law or a corresponding law of another State (s 6A(1)(c)(i))
      `6A(1)(c)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether no matter in that other proceeding is a special federal matter of paragraph (c) or (e) (s 6A(1)(c)(ii))
GIVETH A `Must my proceeding be transferred`
`s 5 -- must my proceeding be transferred` `criminal` `corporations` `pending in` `to go to` `the finding` `6A(1)(a)` `6A(1)(b)` `6A(1)(c)(i)` `6A(1)(c)(ii)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 5`
        IF `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `pending in` AND `the Federal Court or the Family Court` `to go to` AND `federal matter proceeding`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `not required by s 5(1) (s 6A(3), the text's no), but the Supreme Court may transfer it, and it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
                 ELSE `no: s 5(1) does not require it (s 6A(3), the text's no), and the court does not consider transfer appropriate`
        IF `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `pending in` AND `the Federal Court or the Family Court` `to go to`
            THEN IF `more appropriate`
                 THEN `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 5(1))`
                 ELSE `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `pending in` AND `to go to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
                 ELSE `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `between the two WA courts`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Supreme Court and the Family Court of Western Australia, which then has jurisdiction in every matter in it (s 5(4), s 4(6), (7))`
                 ELSE `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `pending in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` AND `to go to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Western Australia`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `yes: that Supreme Court must transfer it to the Supreme Court of Western Australia (s 5(3))`
                 ELSE `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `the Federal Court or the Family Court` `pending in` AND `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `to go to` AND `federal matter proceeding`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `may: s 5(5) does not apply (s 6A(2)), but the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court, and it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
                 ELSE `no: s 5(5) does not apply (s 6A(2), the text's no), and the court does not consider transfer appropriate`
        IF `the Federal Court or the Family Court` `pending in` AND `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `to go to`
            THEN IF `appropriate`
                 THEN `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the Western Australian court you name: the Supreme Court of Western Australia, or the Family Court of Western Australia, which s 3(2) reads into "the Supreme Court" (s 5(5))`
                 ELSE `no: on the court's view s 5 does not require the transfer (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        OTHERWISE `this Act does not provide for that transfer; the cross-vesting Act of the Commonwealth or of another State may (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHERE
        `federal matter proceeding` MEANS `s 6A(1) -- a federal matter proceeding` `6A(1)(a)` `6A(1)(b)` `6A(1)(c)(i)` `6A(1)(c)(ii)`
        -- s 5(4) is excepted from s 3(2): it names both Western Australian courts itself.
        `between the two WA courts` MEANS
                (`pending in` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Western Australia` AND `to go to` EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia`)
            OR  (`pending in` EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia` AND `to go to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Western Australia`)
        -- s 5(1) has no "otherwise in the interests of justice" limb: only "more appropriate" counts.
        `more appropriate` MEANS `the finding` EQUALS `the court finds it more appropriate that the other court decide it`
        -- s 5(2)-(5) limbs (i), (ii) and (iii); and s 6A(2), (3) "considers it appropriate, having regard to the interests
        -- of justice" (fork F4: either finding counts).
        `appropriate` MEANS
                `more appropriate`
            OR  `the finding` EQUALS `the court finds it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide it`

-- s 5(7): where a court transfers a proceeding to another court under a cross-vesting law, and it appears to the first
-- court that another proceeding pending in it arises out of or is related to the first one, and it is in the interests
-- of justice that the other proceeding be determined by the other court, "the first court shall transfer the other
-- proceeding to the other court".
DECLARE `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer your related proceeding to the same court (s 5(7))`
    `no: s 5(7) does not require it (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 5(7)`

@export My proceeding is related to one the court has just transferred to another court under a cross-vesting law. Must mine be transferred to that court too (s 5(7))?
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `the other was transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court your proceeding is in has transferred another proceeding to another court under a cross-vesting law
      `related` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that your proceeding, pending in the same court, arises out of or is related to the transferred one
      `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it in the interests of justice that your proceeding be decided by the court the other one went to
GIVETH A `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one`
`s 5(7) -- must my related proceeding follow` `criminal` `corporations` `the other was transferred` `related` `interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 5(7)`
        IF `the other was transferred` AND `related` AND `interests of justice`
            THEN `yes: the court must transfer your related proceeding to the same court (s 5(7))`
        OTHERWISE `no: s 5(7) does not require it (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

-- s 5(8): "A court may transfer a proceeding under this section on the application of a party to the proceeding, of its
-- own motion or on the application of the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory." s 3(1):
-- "party ... includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding". The list is not closed with "only": someone else gets
-- NOTHING.
DECLARE `My part in the proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `I am the plaintiff, applicant or claimant`
    `I am the defendant or respondent`
    `I have intervened in the proceeding`
    `I am not a party and have not intervened`

DECLARE `Can I apply for a transfer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a party you may apply for the transfer (s 5(8)); the court may also act of its own motion or on an Attorney-General's application`
    `yes: a person who intervenes is a party (s 3(1)), so you may apply for the transfer (s 5(8))`
    `s 5(8) names no application by you: it names a party, the court of its own motion and the Attorneys-General (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I apply to the court to transfer my proceeding under the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) (s 5(8))? A party (including an intervener) may apply; the court may also act of its own motion.
GIVEN `my part` IS A `My part in the proceeding` @desc Your part in the proceeding
GIVETH A `Can I apply for a transfer`
`s 5(8) -- can I apply for a transfer` `my part` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my part` EQUALS `I have intervened in the proceeding`
            THEN `yes: a person who intervenes is a party (s 3(1)), so you may apply for the transfer (s 5(8))`
        IF `my part` EQUALS `I am not a party and have not intervened`
            THEN `s 5(8) names no application by you: it names a party, the court of its own motion and the Attorneys-General (NOTHING-kind)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: as a party you may apply for the transfer (s 5(8)); the court may also act of its own motion or on an Attorney-General's application`

-- s 5(9): a person entitled to practise as a legal practitioner in a court has, "if a proceeding ... in that court is
-- transferred to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction", the same entitlement to
-- practise in the transferred proceeding and any related proceeding determined together with it, in the other court,
-- as if the other court were a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction. An "if": when it fails, the text's no.
DECLARE `Can my lawyer act for me in the court it went to` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your lawyer may practise in the other court in this proceeding as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(9))`
    `s 5(9) gives your lawyer no right on these facts (the text's no: its "if" fails); the other court's own rules decide`

@export My proceeding was transferred to another court. Can my lawyer, who could act for me in the first court, act for me in the new one (s 5(9))?
GIVEN `entitled in the first court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your lawyer is entitled to practise as a legal practitioner in the court the proceeding was in
      `transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding was transferred from that court to another court under a cross-vesting law
      `this or a related one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding your lawyer would act in is the transferred one, or one related to it that is to be decided together with it
GIVETH A `Can my lawyer act for me in the court it went to`
`s 5(9) -- can my lawyer act for me in the new court` `entitled in the first court` `transferred` `this or a related one` MEANS
    IF `entitled in the first court` AND `transferred` AND `this or a related one`
    THEN `yes: your lawyer may practise in the other court in this proceeding as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(9))`
    ELSE `s 5(9) gives your lawyer no right on these facts (the text's no: its "if" fails); the other court's own rules decide`

§§ `s 6 -- Special federal matters`

-- s 6(1): "If (a) a matter for determination in a proceeding that is pending in the Supreme Court is a special federal
-- matter; and (b) the court does not make an order under subsection (3) ..., the court shall transfer the proceeding ...
-- to the Federal Court or a court mentioned in subsection (2)(b)." s 6(1a): only so much as is within that court's
-- jurisdiction. s 6(2): paragraphs (a)-(e) to the Federal Court; paragraph (ba) to whichever of the Family Court, the
-- State Family Court or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory appears appropriate. "Special federal matter" has the
-- meaning in the Commonwealth Act: borrowed, asked as a fact (with its paragraph).
DECLARE `Which kind of special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth Act's definition`
    `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth Act's definition`
    `paragraph (ba) of the Commonwealth Act's definition`
    `no special federal matter`

DECLARE `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court -- so much of it as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to whichever of the Family Court of Australia, the Family Court of Western Australia and the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory appears appropriate -- so much of it as is within that court's jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(b))`
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(1)(b), (3); the text's no)`
    `no: s 6 does not apply to this appeal in the Court of Appeal, because the court below made an order under s 6(3) (s 6(9), the text's no)`
    `not required: s 6 does not require the transfer of a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a Western Australian court (s 6A(3), the text's no); the Supreme Court may still transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    `s 6 does not arise: no special federal matter, or the proceeding is not pending in the Supreme Court (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 6`

@export My proceeding in the Supreme Court of Western Australia includes a "special federal matter". Must it be transferred to a federal court, and to which one (s 6, s 6A)?
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `pending in` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in now
      `the kind` IS A `Which kind of special federal matter` @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter as defined in s 3(1) of the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act, and under which paragraph of that definition
      `ordered kept` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the proceeding itself
      `appeal after an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal instituted in the Court of Appeal from a court that made an order under s 6(3) (or the former s 6(1)) about this special federal matter
      `6A(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves functions or powers given to a Commonwealth authority or officer by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act's definition of enactment (s 6A(1)(b))
      `6A(1)(c)(i)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding arises out of or relates to another proceeding pending in a Western Australian court that arises (or substantially arises) under that State law or a corresponding law of another State (s 6A(1)(c)(i))
      `6A(1)(c)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether no matter in that other proceeding is a special federal matter of paragraph (c) or (e) (s 6A(1)(c)(ii))
GIVETH A `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court`
`s 6 -- must my special federal matter go to a federal court` `criminal` `corporations` `pending in` `the kind` `ordered kept` `appeal after an order` `6A(1)(b)` `6A(1)(c)(i)` `6A(1)(c)(ii)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 6`
        IF (NOT (`the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `pending in`)) OR `the kind` EQUALS `no special federal matter`
            THEN `s 6 does not arise: no special federal matter, or the proceeding is not pending in the Supreme Court (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `appeal after an order`
            THEN `no: s 6 does not apply to this appeal in the Court of Appeal, because the court below made an order under s 6(3) (s 6(9), the text's no)`
        IF `s 6A(1) -- a federal matter proceeding` (`the kind` EQUALS `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth Act's definition`) `6A(1)(b)` `6A(1)(c)(i)` `6A(1)(c)(ii)`
            THEN `not required: s 6 does not require the transfer of a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a Western Australian court (s 6A(3), the text's no); the Supreme Court may still transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
        IF `ordered kept`
            THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(1)(b), (3); the text's no)`
        IF `the kind` EQUALS `paragraph (ba) of the Commonwealth Act's definition`
            THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to whichever of the Family Court of Australia, the Family Court of Western Australia and the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory appears appropriate -- so much of it as is within that court's jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court -- so much of it as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(a))`

-- s 6(3): "The Supreme Court may order that the proceeding be determined by that court if it is satisfied that there are
-- special reasons for doing so ... other than reasons relevant to the convenience of the parties." s 6(4): "Before making
-- an order under subsection (3), the court has to be satisfied that (a) a written notice specifying the nature of the
-- special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the
-- State; and (b) a reasonable time has elapsed ...". "If" and "before ... has to be satisfied": failing, the text's no.
DECLARE `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it must weigh the general rule that special federal matters go to the federal courts, and any Attorney-General's submission (s 6(6))`
    `no: not before written notice of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth and the State Attorney-General (s 6(4)(a), the text's no); the court may adjourn and direct a party to give it (s 6(5))`
    `no: not before a reasonable time has passed since the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b), the text's no)`
    `no: the court is not satisfied that there are special reasons, other than the parties' convenience, for it to decide the proceeding (s 6(3), the text's no)`
    `s 6(3) does not arise: no special federal matter pending in the Supreme Court (the text's no)`

@export Can the Supreme Court of Western Australia keep my proceeding that includes a special federal matter, instead of transferring it (s 6(3)-(6))? Only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience, and only after written notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time for them to respond.
GIVEN `special federal matter pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding in the Supreme Court of Western Australia is a special federal matter
      `Commonwealth notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether written notice of the nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth
      `State notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether written notice of the nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of Western Australia
      `reasonable time passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that a reasonable time has passed since the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions
      `special reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied there are special reasons, in the particular circumstances, for it to decide the proceeding itself, other than reasons about the parties' convenience
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter`
`s 6(3) -- can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` `special federal matter pending` `Commonwealth notified` `State notified` `reasonable time passed` `special reasons` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `special federal matter pending`
            THEN `s 6(3) does not arise: no special federal matter pending in the Supreme Court (the text's no)`
        IF NOT (`Commonwealth notified` AND `State notified`)
            THEN `no: not before written notice of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth and the State Attorney-General (s 6(4)(a), the text's no); the court may adjourn and direct a party to give it (s 6(5))`
        IF NOT `reasonable time passed`
            THEN `no: not before a reasonable time has passed since the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b), the text's no)`
        IF NOT `special reasons`
            THEN `no: the court is not satisfied that there are special reasons, other than the parties' convenience, for it to decide the proceeding (s 6(3), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it must weigh the general rule that special federal matters go to the federal courts, and any Attorney-General's submission (s 6(6))`

-- s 6(5)(b): the court "may direct a party to the proceeding to give a notice in accordance with" s 6(4). The Act sets
-- no time; the direction does. The party's duty is a regulative rule: give the written notice to both Attorneys-General
-- within the days the court allowed (fork F7: the last day counts).
DECLARE `Directed party` IS ONE OF `the directed party`
DECLARE `Notice step` IS ONE OF `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Directed party` `Notice step`
`s 6(5)(b) -- give the notice within the time the court directed` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the directed party`
    MUST `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the directed party` BECAUSE "the notice under s 6(4) was not given within the time the Supreme Court directed under s 6(5)(b)"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. A CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH on the stable l4, so
-- FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(5)(b) -- notice on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 6(5)(b) -- give the notice within the time the court directed` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the directed party` `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Have I given the notice the court directed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court directed (s 6(5)(b)); s 6(4)(a) is met once both Attorneys-General have it`
    `not yet, but still in time: the time the court directed has not run out (s 6(5)(b))`
    `no: the notice was not given within the time the court directed (s 6(5)(b)); until both Attorneys-General have it, the Supreme Court cannot order that it keep the proceeding (s 6(4))`

@export The Supreme Court directed me to give the Attorneys-General written notice of the special federal matter in my proceeding (s 6(5)(b)). Have I done so in time?
GIVEN `directed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the court gave the direction
      `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the court's direction allowed you to give the notice (the Act itself sets no time)
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the written notice to both the Commonwealth and the State Attorney-General
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Have I given the notice the court directed`
`s 6(5)(b) -- have I given the notice in time` `directed on` `the days allowed` `given` `given on` `today` MEANS
    IF `given`
    THEN IF `s 6(5)(b) -- notice on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day given`
         THEN `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court directed (s 6(5)(b)); s 6(4)(a) is met once both Attorneys-General have it`
         ELSE `no: the notice was not given within the time the court directed (s 6(5)(b)); until both Attorneys-General have it, the Supreme Court cannot order that it keep the proceeding (s 6(4))`
    ELSE IF `the days from` `directed on` `to` `today` GREATER THAN `the days allowed`
         THEN `no: the notice was not given within the time the court directed (s 6(5)(b)); until both Attorneys-General have it, the Supreme Court cannot order that it keep the proceeding (s 6(4))`
         ELSE `not yet, but still in time: the time the court directed has not run out (s 6(5)(b))`
    WHERE
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `directed on` `to` `given on`
        -- A notice given before the direction is counted as given on day 0 (in time).
        `the day given` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

-- s 6(7): "Nothing in this section prevents the court granting urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in the
-- interests of justice to do so." The "if" failing: the text's no. Relief that is not urgent and interlocutory lies
-- outside the subsection's own wording: NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief` IS ONE OF
    `yes: nothing in s 6 stops the Supreme Court granting urgent interlocutory relief, as it is in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
    `s 6(7) does not save it: the court does not find the relief in the interests of justice (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 6(7) does not reach it: it speaks only of urgent relief of an interlocutory nature (NOTHING-kind)`

@export My proceeding includes a special federal matter that must go to a federal court. Can the Supreme Court of Western Australia still give me urgent interim relief first (s 6(7))?
GIVEN `urgent and interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the relief you seek is urgent and of an interlocutory (interim) nature, such as an urgent injunction
      `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds it in the interests of justice to grant it
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief`
`s 6(7) -- can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief` `urgent and interlocutory` `interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `urgent and interlocutory`
            THEN `s 6(7) does not reach it: it speaks only of urgent relief of an interlocutory nature (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `interests of justice`
            THEN `yes: nothing in s 6 stops the Supreme Court granting urgent interlocutory relief, as it is in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
        OTHERWISE `s 6(7) does not save it: the court does not find the relief in the interests of justice (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

-- s 6(8): if the Supreme Court "through inadvertence" determines a special federal matter proceeding without an s 6(3)
-- order, or after an s 6(3) order made "through inadvertence" without the s 6(4) notice, "nothing in this section
-- invalidates the decision". s 7(8): where the Court of Appeal determines a proceeding to which s 7(5) applies, as
-- mentioned in s 7(7) or through inadvertence, "nothing in this section invalidates the decision". Outside those words
-- the Act is silent: NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `What happened in my case` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without an order under s 6(3)`
    `the Supreme Court decided it after an order under s 6(3) made without the s 6(4) notice`
    `the Court of Appeal decided an appeal that s 7(5) sends to a Full Court of the Federal Court or the Family Court`

DECLARE `Is the decision invalid because the case was not transferred` IS ONE OF
    `not invalid for that reason: nothing in s 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
    `not invalid for that reason: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(8))`
    `this Act does not say: s 6(8) and s 7(8) save only a decision made through inadvertence or, for s 7, after the Court of Appeal went on under s 7(7) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export A court decided my case although the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) required it to go to a federal court. Is the decision invalid for that reason (s 6(8), s 7(8))?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened in my case` @desc What the court did
      `inadvertence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court did it through inadvertence (by oversight)
      `went on under s 7(7)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the Court of Appeal only: whether it went on to decide the appeal because the interests of justice required it (s 7(7))
GIVETH A `Is the decision invalid because the case was not transferred`
`s 6(8), s 7(8) -- is the decision invalid because it was not transferred` `what happened` `inadvertence` `went on under s 7(7)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the Court of Appeal` AND (`inadvertence` OR `went on under s 7(7)`)
            THEN `not invalid for that reason: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(8))`
        IF (NOT `the Court of Appeal`) AND `inadvertence`
            THEN `not invalid for that reason: nothing in s 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
        OTHERWISE `this Act does not say: s 6(8) and s 7(8) save only a decision made through inadvertence or, for s 7, after the Court of Appeal went on under s 7(7) (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHERE
        `the Court of Appeal` MEANS `what happened` EQUALS `the Court of Appeal decided an appeal that s 7(5) sends to a Full Court of the Federal Court or the Family Court`

§§ `s 7 -- Institution and hearing of appeals`

-- s 7(1): no appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Court of Appeal. s 7(2): none
-- from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the other. s 7(3): an appeal from a single judge of the Supreme Court in
-- which the only matters are not under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act goes "only" to the Court of Appeal.
-- s 7(4): none from a court of summary jurisdiction to the Supreme Court if an appeal lies to the State Family Court.
-- s 7(5): where a matter arises under a Schedule Act, "only" to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family
-- Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court. s 7(6): an appeal from a judge of a State Family
-- Court with a Schedule Act matter and another matter "may be dealt with as if" no matter arose under a Schedule Act.
DECLARE `The decision I want to appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court`
    `a decision of a single judge of the Family Court of Australia`
    `a decision of a single judge of the Supreme Court of Western Australia`
    `a decision of a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia`
    `a decision of a court of summary jurisdiction`
    `some other decision`

DECLARE `The court I want to appeal to` IS ONE OF
    `the Court of Appeal of Western Australia`
    `the Full Court of the Federal Court`
    `the Full Court of the Family Court of Australia`
    `the High Court, with special leave`
    `the Supreme Court of Western Australia, other than the Court of Appeal`
    `some other court`

DECLARE `Where can my appeal go` IS ONE OF
    `no: an appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court does not lie to the Court of Appeal (s 7(1), the text's no)`
    `no: an appeal does not lie from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the other of them (s 7(2), the text's no)`
    `yes: with no matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal is instituted in the Court of Appeal and only there (s 7(3))`
    `no: with no matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal must be instituted only in the Court of Appeal (s 7(3), the text's no)`
    `yes: with a matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal goes only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(5))`
    `no: with a matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal must go to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(5), the text's no)`
    `no: no appeal to the Supreme Court from a court of summary jurisdiction where an appeal lies to the Family Court of Western Australia (s 7(4), the text's no)`
    `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(6))`
    `this Act does not say where this appeal goes (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 7`

@export Where can my appeal be brought under the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (WA) (s 7)? It depends on the court below, and on whether a matter in the appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act.
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the appeal is in a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `from` IS A `The decision I want to appeal` @desc The decision you want to appeal from
      `to` IS A `The court I want to appeal to` @desc The court you want to appeal to
      `Schedule Act matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in the appeal arises under an Act specified in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act
      `another matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the appeal also involves another matter, not under a Schedule Act
      `appeal lies to the Family Court of WA` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a decision of a court of summary jurisdiction: whether an appeal lies from it to the Family Court of Western Australia
GIVETH A `Where can my appeal go`
`s 7 -- where can my appeal go` `criminal` `corporations` `from` `to` `Schedule Act matter` `another matter` `appeal lies to the Family Court of WA` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 7`
        IF `from a single federal judge` AND `to` EQUALS `the Court of Appeal of Western Australia`
            THEN `no: an appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court does not lie to the Court of Appeal (s 7(1), the text's no)`
        IF `across the federal courts`
            THEN `no: an appeal does not lie from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the other of them (s 7(2), the text's no)`
        IF `from a WA superior court judge` AND (NOT `Schedule Act matter`)
            THEN IF `to` EQUALS `the Court of Appeal of Western Australia`
                 THEN `yes: with no matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal is instituted in the Court of Appeal and only there (s 7(3))`
                 ELSE `no: with no matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal must be instituted only in the Court of Appeal (s 7(3), the text's no)`
        -- s 7(5) excludes "a proceeding to which subsection (6) applies": s 7(6) is answered first.
        IF `from` EQUALS `a decision of a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia` AND `Schedule Act matter` AND `another matter`
            THEN `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(6))`
        IF `from a WA superior court judge`
            THEN IF `to a Full Court or the High Court`
                 THEN `yes: with a matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal goes only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(5))`
                 ELSE `no: with a matter under a Schedule Act, the appeal must go to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, or with special leave to the High Court (s 7(5), the text's no)`
        IF `from` EQUALS `a decision of a court of summary jurisdiction` AND `appeal lies to the Family Court of WA` AND `to the Supreme Court`
            THEN `no: no appeal to the Supreme Court from a court of summary jurisdiction where an appeal lies to the Family Court of Western Australia (s 7(4), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `this Act does not say where this appeal goes (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHERE
        -- s 7(3) and (5) are not excepted from s 3(2): "a single judge of the Supreme Court" includes a judge of the
        -- Family Court of Western Australia (fork F2, fork F18).
        `from a WA superior court judge` MEANS
                `from` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Supreme Court of Western Australia`
            OR  `from` EQUALS `a decision of a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia`
        `from a single federal judge` MEANS
                `from` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court`
            OR  `from` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Family Court of Australia`
        `across the federal courts` MEANS
                (`from` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court` AND `to` EQUALS `the Full Court of the Family Court of Australia`)
            OR  (`from` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Family Court of Australia` AND `to` EQUALS `the Full Court of the Federal Court`)
        -- s 7(5) "as the case requires": either Full Court is accepted (fork F8).
        `to a Full Court or the High Court` MEANS
                `to` EQUALS `the Full Court of the Federal Court`
            OR  `to` EQUALS `the Full Court of the Family Court of Australia`
            OR  `to` EQUALS `the High Court, with special leave`
        -- s 7(4) "to the Supreme Court": the Court of Appeal is taken as part of the Supreme Court (fork F11).
        `to the Supreme Court` MEANS
                `to` EQUALS `the Supreme Court of Western Australia, other than the Court of Appeal`
            OR  `to` EQUALS `the Court of Appeal of Western Australia`

-- s 7(7): where the Court of Appeal commences to hear an appeal and, before determining it, it appears that s 7(5)
-- applies, "the Court shall, unless the interests of justice require that the Court proceed to determine the proceeding,
-- transfer the proceeding to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court". Fork F9: the "unless" once met
-- leaves the Court free to go on (its decision then saved by s 7(8)).
DECLARE `Must the Court of Appeal send my appeal to a Full Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Court of Appeal must transfer it to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(7))`
    `no: the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on and decide it (s 7(7)); its decision is not invalid under s 7 (s 7(8))`
    `s 7(7) does not arise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export The Court of Appeal has begun to hear my appeal, and it now appears that it should have gone to a Full Court of the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 7(5)). Must the Court of Appeal send it there (s 7(7))?
GIVEN `hearing begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court of Appeal has begun to hear your appeal
      `s 7(5) applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before deciding the appeal, it appears to the Court that s 7(5) applies (a matter in it arises under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act)
      `interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on and decide the appeal itself
GIVETH A `Must the Court of Appeal send my appeal to a Full Court`
`s 7(7) -- must the Court of Appeal send my appeal to a Full Court` `hearing begun` `s 7(5) applies` `interests of justice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`hearing begun` AND `s 7(5) applies`)
            THEN `s 7(7) does not arise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `interests of justice`
            THEN `no: the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on and decide it (s 7(7)); its decision is not invalid under s 7 (s 7(8))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Court of Appeal must transfer it to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(7))`

§§ `s 8 -- Orders by Supreme Court`

-- s 8(1): where a proceeding is pending in "a court, other than the Supreme Court, of the State" or "a tribunal
-- established by or under an Act", and it appears to the Supreme Court that (i) it is related to a proceeding pending in
-- the Federal Court, the Family Court or a Supreme Court of another State or Territory and, if removed, there would be
-- grounds to transfer that other proceeding to the Supreme Court, or (ii) an order should be made so that a transfer can
-- be considered, "the Supreme Court may, on the application of a party ... or of its own motion, make an order removing
-- the relevant proceeding to the Supreme Court". s 8(2): the Act then applies as if it were pending in the Supreme Court.
-- s 8(3): the Supreme Court may remit it. By s 3(2) "the Supreme Court" here includes the Family Court of Western
-- Australia (fork F10).
DECLARE `Where my proceeding is now` IS ONE OF
    `a Western Australian court other than the Supreme Court and the Family Court of Western Australia`
    `a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    `the Supreme Court or the Family Court of Western Australia, or a court outside Western Australia`

DECLARE `Can my proceeding be removed to the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may order it removed to itself, on your application as a party or of its own motion (s 8(1)); this Act then treats it as pending in the Supreme Court (s 8(2)), which may later send it back (s 8(3))`
    `no: s 8 does not allow removal on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 8`

@export My proceeding is in a lower Western Australian court or a tribunal. Can it be removed into the Supreme Court of Western Australia so that it can be heard with, or transferred to, another court (s 8)?
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `where now` IS A `Where my proceeding is now` @desc Where your proceeding is now
      `related elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court finds your proceeding related to one pending in the Federal Court, the Family Court or a Supreme Court of another State or Territory, which could be transferred to the Supreme Court if yours were removed (s 8(1)(b)(i))
      `to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court finds that an order should be made so that it can consider transferring your proceeding to another court (s 8(1)(b)(ii))
GIVETH A `Can my proceeding be removed to the Supreme Court`
`s 8 -- can my proceeding be removed to the Supreme Court` `criminal` `corporations` `where now` `related elsewhere` `to consider a transfer` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 8`
        IF (NOT (`where now` EQUALS `the Supreme Court or the Family Court of Western Australia, or a court outside Western Australia`)) AND (`related elsewhere` OR `to consider a transfer`)
            THEN `yes: the Supreme Court may order it removed to itself, on your application as a party or of its own motion (s 8(1)); this Act then treats it as pending in the Supreme Court (s 8(2)), which may later send it back (s 8(3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: s 8 does not allow removal on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

§§ `s 10 -- Matters under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act`

-- s 10: where (a) the proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court or a Supreme Court of another State or Territory; (b) a
-- matter in it arises under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974; (c) no matter in it is a special
-- federal matter; (d) it is not an appeal from a judgment of a court; and (e) a court of the State, other than the
-- Supreme Court, has jurisdiction in all of its matters, "the court ... may, on the application of a party ... or of its
-- own motion, transfer the proceeding to the court referred to in paragraph (e)". s 10 is not excepted from s 3(2): "the
-- Supreme Court" in (a) and (e) includes the Family Court of Western Australia (fork F2), so a proceeding in that court
-- can be sent down, and in (e) the lower court is one other than the Supreme Court and the Family Court of WA.
DECLARE `Can my consumer protection case go to a lower court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court it is in may transfer it to that other Western Australian court, on your application as a party or of its own motion (s 10)`
    `no: s 10 does not allow it on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 10`

@export My consumer protection claim under Part V Division 1 or 1A of the Trade Practices Act 1974 is in a Supreme Court (or the Family Court of Western Australia). Can it be sent down to a lower Western Australian court (s 10)?
GIVEN `criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a criminal proceeding
      `corporations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jurisdiction is the corporations jurisdiction dealt with by Part 9.6A Division 1 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)
      `in a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court of Western Australia, the Family Court of Western Australia (read in by s 3(2)) or a Supreme Court of another State or Territory
      `Part V matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in it arises under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974 (Cth)
      `special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any matter in it is a special federal matter
      `an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal from a judgment of a court
      `lower court has all of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Western Australian court other than the Supreme Court and the Family Court of Western Australia has jurisdiction in every matter in your proceeding
GIVETH A `Can my consumer protection case go to a lower court`
`s 10 -- can my consumer protection case go to a lower court` `criminal` `corporations` `in a Supreme Court` `Part V matter` `special federal matter` `an appeal` `lower court has all of it` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `outside this Act` `criminal` `corporations`
            THEN `not under this Act: a criminal proceeding (s 3(1)) or the Corporations Act jurisdiction (s 3A) (the text's no) -- s 10`
        IF `in a Supreme Court` AND `Part V matter` AND (NOT `special federal matter`) AND (NOT `an appeal`) AND `lower court has all of it`
            THEN `yes: the court it is in may transfer it to that other Western Australian court, on your application as a party or of its own motion (s 10)`
        OTHERWISE `no: s 10 does not allow it on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

§§ `s 11 and s 12 -- Conduct of proceedings and costs`

-- s 11(1): where a court will, or is likely to, be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction in deciding a matter: (a) it
-- applies the law in force where it sits, including choice of law rules; (b) but where the matter is "a right of action
-- arising under a written law of another State or Territory", the written and unwritten law of that State or Territory;
-- (c) rules of evidence and procedure as it considers appropriate, being rules applied in a superior court in Australia
-- or an external Territory.
DECLARE `Which law the court applies to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules a superior court in Australia or an external Territory applies (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court is sitting, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules a superior court in Australia or an external Territory applies (s 11(1)(c))`
    `s 11(1) does not apply: the court will not be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction (the text's no: its "where" fails); this Act does not say which law applies`

@export Which law will the court apply to my claim when it hears it under cross-vested jurisdiction (s 11(1))? Usually the law where the court sits, including its choice of law rules; but a right of action under a written law of another State or Territory is decided under that place's law.
GIVEN `cross-vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court will, or is likely to, be exercising jurisdiction given by this Act or another cross-vesting law (for example, after your proceeding was transferred to it)
      `right under another State's written law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a right of action arising under a written law (a statute) of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits
GIVETH A `Which law the court applies to my claim`
`s 11(1) -- which law will the court apply to my claim` `cross-vested` `right under another State's written law` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `cross-vested`
            THEN `s 11(1) does not apply: the court will not be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction (the text's no: its "where" fails); this Act does not say which law applies`
        IF `right under another State's written law`
            THEN `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules a superior court in Australia or an external Territory applies (s 11(1)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court is sitting, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules a superior court in Australia or an external Territory applies (s 11(1)(c))`

-- s 11(3): where a proceeding is transferred or removed, the transferee court deals with it as if, subject to its own
-- orders, the steps taken in the transferor court (including orders) had been taken in the transferee court.
DECLARE `Do the steps already taken count in the new court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the new court deals with the proceeding as if the steps taken in the old court, including its orders, had been taken in the new court, subject to any order of the new court (s 11(3))`
    `s 11(3) does not apply: the proceeding was not transferred or removed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export My proceeding was transferred or removed to another court. Do the steps already taken and orders already made still count there (s 11(3))?
GIVEN `transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was transferred or removed from one court to another
GIVETH A `Do the steps already taken count in the new court`
`s 11(3) -- do the steps already taken count in the new court` `transferred or removed` MEANS
    IF `transferred or removed`
    THEN `yes: the new court deals with the proceeding as if the steps taken in the old court, including its orders, had been taken in the new court, subject to any order of the new court (s 11(3))`
    ELSE `s 11(3) does not apply: the proceeding was not transferred or removed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

-- s 12: "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court, that court may make an order as to costs that relate
-- to the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal if those costs have not already been dealt with by
-- another court."
DECLARE `Can the new court order the costs of the earlier stage` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the new court may make an order about the costs of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`
    `no: those costs have already been dealt with by another court (s 12, the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 12 does not apply: the proceeding was not transferred or removed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export My proceeding was transferred or removed to another court. Can the new court make an order about the costs of the proceeding before the transfer (s 12)?
GIVEN `transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was transferred or removed to the court that would make the order
      `already dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs of the earlier stage have already been dealt with by another court
GIVETH A `Can the new court order the costs of the earlier stage`
`s 12 -- can the new court order the costs of the earlier stage` `transferred or removed` `already dealt with` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `transferred or removed`
            THEN `s 12 does not apply: the proceeding was not transferred or removed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `already dealt with`
            THEN `no: those costs have already been dealt with by another court (s 12, the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the new court may make an order about the costs of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`

§§ `s 13 and s 14 -- Appeals from transfer decisions, and enforcement`

-- s 13: "An appeal does not lie from a decision of a court (a) in relation to the transfer or removal of a proceeding
-- under this Act; or (b) as to which rules of evidence and procedure are to be applied pursuant to section 11(1)." A flat
-- negative: the text's no. Other decisions: s 13 is silent (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `The decision I want to challenge` IS ONE OF
    `a decision about transferring or removing my proceeding under this Act`
    `a decision under s 11(1) about which rules of evidence and procedure apply`
    `some other decision in my proceeding`

DECLARE `Can I appeal that decision` IS ONE OF
    `no: no appeal lies from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a), the text's no)`
    `no: no appeal lies from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b), the text's no)`
    `s 13 does not bar an appeal from it; whether one lies is for other law (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I appeal the court's decision to transfer (or not to transfer) my proceeding, or its choice of rules of evidence and procedure (s 13)?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to challenge` @desc The decision you want to appeal
GIVETH A `Can I appeal that decision`
`s 13 -- can I appeal that decision` `the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision about transferring or removing my proceeding under this Act`
            THEN `no: no appeal lies from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a), the text's no)`
        IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision under s 11(1) about which rules of evidence and procedure apply`
            THEN `no: no appeal lies from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `s 13 does not bar an appeal from it; whether one lies is for other law (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 14(2): "A judgment of the Supreme Court that is given, in whole or in part, in the exercise of jurisdiction conferred
-- by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is enforceable in the State as if the judgment had been
-- given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court apart from any such law." By s 3(2) "the
-- Supreme Court" includes the Family Court of Western Australia. Other judgments: s 14(2) is silent (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Can I enforce the judgment in Western Australia` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the judgment is enforceable in Western Australia as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
    `s 14(2) does not reach it: it covers only a judgment of the Supreme Court (or the Family Court) of Western Australia given in whole or in part in cross-vested jurisdiction (NOTHING-kind)`

@export I have a judgment of the Supreme Court of Western Australia given in cross-vested jurisdiction. Can I enforce it in Western Australia as an ordinary Supreme Court judgment (s 14(2))?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court that gave the judgment
      `cross-vested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment was given, in whole or in part, in jurisdiction given by this Act or another cross-vesting law
GIVETH A `Can I enforce the judgment in Western Australia`
`s 14(2) -- can I enforce the judgment in Western Australia` `the court` `cross-vested` MEANS
    IF `the Supreme Court, read with s 3(2)` `the court` AND `cross-vested`
    THEN `yes: the judgment is enforceable in Western Australia as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
    ELSE `s 14(2) does not reach it: it covers only a judgment of the Supreme Court (or the Family Court) of Western Australia given in whole or in part in cross-vested jurisdiction (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 14(3): "Where (a) a provision of a law of the State (not being a law relating to the enforcement of judgments)
-- refers to a thing done by the Supreme Court; and (b) that thing is done by another court in exercise of jurisdiction
-- conferred by this Act, the reference in that provision to the Supreme Court shall be read as a reference to that other
-- court." A "where": when it fails, the text's no; the bracketed exclusion is the text's no too.
DECLARE `Does a WA law that speaks of the Supreme Court cover what the other court did` IS ONE OF
    `yes: that provision is read as referring to the court that did the thing (s 14(3))`
    `no: s 14(3) does not reach a law relating to the enforcement of judgments (s 14(3)(a), the text's no)`
    `s 14(3) does not apply on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export A Western Australian law gives me something when the Supreme Court does a thing (for example, makes an order). Another court did that thing in jurisdiction given by this Act. Does the law treat that court's act as if the Supreme Court had done it (s 14(3))?
GIVEN `refers to the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a provision of a Western Australian law refers to a thing done by the Supreme Court
      `enforcement law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that law is a law relating to the enforcement of judgments
      `done by another court under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that thing was done by another court in the exercise of jurisdiction conferred by this Act (for example, a Supreme Court of another State hearing a Western Australian State matter under s 4(3))
GIVETH A `Does a WA law that speaks of the Supreme Court cover what the other court did`
`s 14(3) -- does a WA law that speaks of the Supreme Court cover the other court` `refers to the Supreme Court` `enforcement law` `done by another court under this Act` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`refers to the Supreme Court` AND `done by another court under this Act`)
            THEN `s 14(3) does not apply on these facts (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
        IF `enforcement law`
            THEN `no: s 14(3) does not reach a law relating to the enforcement of judgments (s 14(3)(a), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: that provision is read as referring to the court that did the thing (s 14(3))`
